# acceptance4lua Tool Spec

Purpose: portable Gherkin acceptance pipeline support for this Lua project.

Reference implementation: `acceptance4lua` entry in `tools/tools.lock` (follows upstream master HEAD); upstream rock CLI lives under `acceptance4lua/cli/` (`parser.lua`, `generator.lua`, `ir_dry.lua`, `mutator.lua`) with the mutator engine under `acceptance4lua/mutator/`.
Project CLI wrappers: `tools/packages/acceptance/cli/parser.lua` (Gherkin → JSON IR), `tools/packages/acceptance/cli/generator.lua` (IR → bound entrypoints), `tools/packages/acceptance/cli/ir_dry.lua` (IR → findings report) and `tools/packages/acceptance_mutate/mutator.lua` (mutator with the project step module defaulted).
Project entrypoints: `lua tools/cli.lua acceptance` (regenerate gitignored generated specs, then run/aggregate) and `lua tools/cli.lua acceptance-mutate` (Gherkin mutation soft lane). Parser, generator, IR-DRY and mutator wrappers are called by their explicit project paths.

CLI contract:
- `lua tools/cli.lua acceptance [--serial|--parallel|--capabilities]` (native serial execution by default; nonempty case counts required in both modes). Parallel execution shares `foundation.parallel_lanes` with verify and behavior/tooling: `cmd start /b` launches Windows PowerShell, whose built-in .NET Add-Type/PInvoke owner creates a suspended cmd process, assigns its exact handle to a private Job Object, then resumes it. Timeout terminates only that Job and waits for all owned writers to close before atomic completion publication. The parent then blocks once on all completion files through a Windows PowerShell waiter (`lane_wait.ps1`, .NET FileSystemWatcher timed wait bounded by the lane timeout); it never spins or polls with a sleep command, and a waiter failure is not retried — unpublished lanes report output_incomplete. Empty output fails. No GCC, SDK/download, title matching, sh, sleep command, Start-Job or Node runtime.
- `--capabilities` reports registered/selected/excluded identities without generation or execution. The retained `features/tooling/acceptance_mutator_status.feature` / `test_acceptance_mutator_status_acceptance.lua` is declared `capability=acceptance-mutate`: native Windows excludes it with `status=unavailable` and an explicit reason, never counts it as passed. Default regeneration and execution use the same selected set.
- `lua tools/cli.lua acceptance-mutate [options]` (upstream mutator flags pass through as trailing args)
- Parser: `lua tools/packages/acceptance/cli/parser.lua <feature> <json-ir>`.
- Generator: `lua tools/packages/acceptance/cli/generator.lua <json-ir> <spec-output>`.
- Mutator: `lua tools/packages/acceptance_mutate/mutator.lua [options]`.
- Exit codes follow `tools/cli.lua` (#300 Q2, extended by #96): 0 ok / 1 business failure / 2 usage error / 3 unavailable.
- Tool bootstrap unavailability (#112): when `bootstrap.ensure_tool("acceptance4lua")` cannot install the tool and no installed version can be reused, the failure is classified by its cause. Only a network/download failure (recognised from the git/luarocks output: host resolution, connect/timeout/reset, TLS/proxy, failed download/fetch) in upstream HEAD resolution `git ls-remote`, source clone `git clone`, or `luarocks make` dependency download returns `nil, err, unavailable` (`foundation.tool_availability` record: `status=unavailable`, `tool`, `step` = `resolve`/`fetch`/`build`, exit code 3). `lua tools/cli.lua spec-lane --profile acceptance|tooling` then exits 3 with `[spec-lane] status=unavailable tool=<name> step=<step>` on stderr, naming the failed step, the likely network cause and the remedy (check network and retry, or pre-install into `.toolcache/luarocks`); no test runs and the summary reports no `failed` count. Everything else stays a real failure (exit 1): repository defects (tool missing from `tools/tools.lock`, unreadable lockfile), a missing upstream repository, compile/build errors of the locked source, and any unrecognised error (conservative: never a false "unavailable"). `lua tools/cli.lua verify [--tooling]` reports a lane that exited 3 as `UNAVAILABLE <lane>`, counts it as `unavailable=K` (never passed, never failed), and exits 1 if any lane really failed, otherwise 3 — never 0. There is no automatic retry and no skip flag for this state.

Windows guarantee boundary (#96, ADR 0066):
- The acceptance pipeline itself (`lua tools/cli.lua acceptance`, plus the explicit parser / generator / IR-DRY entrypoints) is inside the native-Windows guarantee surface and is a required gate.
- All actual mutation entrypoints (`lua tools/cli.lua acceptance-mutate`, `acceptance_mutate/mutate_lane.lua`, and `acceptance_mutate/mutator.lua`) are currently declared a POSIX-only capability. Direct mutator `--help` remains exit 0 and no-argument usage remains exit 2 without installing the tool. On Windows a request is reported as unavailable *before* any tool install or spawn: exit code 3, `status=unavailable` on stderr, no mutator run, no mutation verdict. It must never be reported as a pass, and it must not surface the lane probe's own misdiagnosis (the probe chain — parser → generator → runner_worker — loses the process stdin to subprocesses spawned during bootstrap on native Windows, so `runner_worker` reads no job at all and the lane blames the job response instead of the platform).
- `--skip-if-unavailable` is the explicit opt-in that records the skip: exit code 0, `status=skipped` on stdout. A skip is not a pass; it means no mutation result exists on this platform. The flag is consumed by `foundation.platform_boundary` and is never forwarded upstream.
- Supported subset on native Windows: `--help` (exit 0), the boundary diagnostic, and the whole non-mutation acceptance surface (parser, generator, IR-DRY, `acceptance`). Out of the guarantee surface: any real mutation run, its periodic status output, and its killed/survived/error report.
- Upstream mutator flags: `--feature <path>` (required), `--work-dir`, `--generated-dir`, `--steps-module`, `--workers`, `--timeout`, `--status-interval`, `--level full|hard|soft` (default hard), `--skip-columns <csv>`, `--runner-worker <command>` (required), `--implementation-hash`, `--json`, `--verbose`.
- The mutator supports `--skip-columns <csv>` as the APS step-7 equivalent-mutation filter: named example columns produce no mutation entries. `tools/packages/acceptance_mutate/mutate_lane.lua` passes the project default identity-threading columns (`角色ID,观察角色ID,行动角色ID,预期行动角色ID`, issue #204); assertion/selection columns (`验证*`, `产品ID`) must never be filtered.
- Acceptance tests for this repo are run through `lua tools/cli.lua acceptance`, never by running generated suites bare.
- Default tooling excludes only the explicitly declared POSIX engine integration files `tools/packages/acceptance_mutate/test/test_mutator_level.lua` (12 engine cases; its two pure level-validation cases remain selected in test_mutator_status_format.lua) and `tools/packages/acceptance_mutate/test/test_mutator_status.lua` (2 cases), printing each full identity, capability and reason. The files are retained; an explicit runner request exits 3 before dependency install/spawn. Generic status-format/report and public step protocol tests remain native gates. This capability partition is separate from test-framework backend routing.
- Missing, invalid or empty manifests are infrastructure errors; explicit empty regeneration entries return nil/error and capability listing cannot report an empty success.
- IR-DRY: `lua tools/packages/acceptance/cli/ir_dry.lua <json-ir> <report-json>` (optional `--include-exact`; exit 2 usage / 1 failure). It was reinstated as a project entrypoint after the data/driver/lane split removed the old one — the rock ships `acceptance4lua/cli/ir_dry.lua`. It writes a findings report (duplicate step shapes, placeholder variants, identical example columns); it never rewrites the feature, so pruning stays a specifier decision.
- One interpreter discipline across the acceptance surface: `mutate_lane.lua` (parser / generator / runner_worker / mutator spawns) and `acceptance/steps/mutator_status.lua` use `foundation.lua54` (`LUA54_BIN` overrides are version-validated; missing or non-5.4 interpreters fail explicitly), matching `acceptance/spawn_env.lua` and the project's LuaRocks 5.4 tree.

APS contract:
- Follow the Acceptance-Pipeline-Specification (upstream https://github.com/unclebob/Acceptance-Pipeline-Specification).
- The parser writes canonical JSON IR for the supported deterministic Gherkin subset.
- The project entrypoint emits genuine busted describe/it specs through runtime.define_specs, with embedded IR and optional ACCEPTANCE_FEATURE_JSON override; generated specs never install a private harness or exit. The isolated executor verifies metadata hashes and scenario/example ordinal identities, returns JSON counts and execution identities, and fails on empty/incomplete execution. The persistent worker keeps JSON-lines stdout, flushes every response, isolates each job in a child process, and distinguishes test_failure from infrastructure_error while continuing after malformed jobs. Native foundation writes keep output local. Metadata filenames use a full SHA-256 of the original feature identity to bound Windows path length; metadata preserves the complete feature_path, ir_path, generated_files and generated-files-only implementation_hash. An existing malformed or mismatched identity rejects generation before overwriting the entry. Upstream/cache sources are unchanged.
- The mutator changes Gherkin example values, runs mutated IR through a persistent runner adapter, emits periodic status to stderr, and reports killed/survived/error results.

Runtime dispatch requirement:
- The runtime must match a step by pattern, capture the placeholder *name*, and read that key from the current example object. It must not require one handler registration per placeholder name.
- Rationale: one scenario often refers to several distinct instances of the same thing (`<槽位>`, `<新槽位>`, `<验证槽位>`), so the same step shape must carry different example columns. Exact-step-text dispatch forces a duplicate handler registration for every such name.
- APS names this as the recommended default: "the recommended project runtime default is regex or expression matching that captures the placeholder name and reads that key from the current example object."
- Feature files are correct as written; this is a runtime concern. Do not rewrite Gherkin to work around dispatch.

Project boundary:
- Chinese normalization, generated-spec location (`tools/packages/acceptance/generated/`, gitignored), step handlers, runtime fixtures, runner adapter, and convenience scripts remain project-specific.
- Gherkin acceptance mutation scenario manifests live in feature files (`# acceptance-mutation-manifest-begin` blocks) and must not be hand-edited; the mutator rewrites them as part of its runs.
- Versioned mutation survivor baselines previously lived under `features/survivors/` (moved out of the acceptance engine's directory by the testing-simplification decision's data-plane split); the #293 ledger concluded and the directory was removed per the #430 cleanup map (full history retained in git).
